Document 3: IrwinOS SSD Portable Boot Architecture (v1)
What This Was
The IrwinOS SSD boot architecture was designed to make the entire Irwin system portable and host-agnostic — a self-contained AI operating system that could boot from a single SSD on any machine, Mac, Linux, or Windows via VM. The goal was reproducibility and independence from any specific host machine configuration.
Key innovations in this design:
Three strict data planes (prod, test, quarantine) with no cross-contamination
Dependency-ordered engine initialization
Sandbox isolation enforced at the mount namespace level
Model tier routing built into the boot sequence
All configuration stored inside the system — no host OS path dependencies
The boot sequence (SSD detection → OS init → bootstrap service → model router → engine initialization → user shell) established the pattern that the current AxiomOS boot architecture follows.
Why the Architecture Evolved Past This
The SSD portable architecture solved the wrong problem elegantly. Making a userspace application portable across host machines is a legitimate engineering challenge. But the deeper problem — that the constitutional governance layer existed only as markdown files that could be ignored by any sufficiently privileged process — wasn't addressed by portability.
A constitutional OS that runs from an SSD but enforces its rules only at the application layer is still an application, not an OS. The governance is still a suggestion.
The current architecture addresses this by moving the constitutional layer to the kernel itself. The result is an OS that is not just portable but structurally governed — the rules are enforced at the level where all computation occurs, not in a folder that boots alongside the system.
What Replaced It
The AxiomOS kernel architecture replaces the SSD portable boot design. The three data lake separation (prod/test/quarantine) maps directly to the current dual data lake architecture with hardware-keyed private lake. The engine initialization order maps to the six-component build sequence. The sandbox isolation moves from mount namespace to constitutional kernel enforcement.
The boot philosophy — structured, ordered, verified — survives entirely in the current design.